2.2 Statistical challenges
Biological growth data frequently present characteristics that are not adequately represented by ordinary nonlinear least squares, including:

increasing or decreasing residual variability;
repeated observations from the same experimental unit;
between-individual variation in growth parameters;
serial correlation between observations;
incomplete or irregular measurement schedules.

PGM therefore includes variance functions, nonlinear mixed-effects models, and residual correlation structures.

2.3 Documentation conventions
Throughout this documentation:

\(W(t)\) denotes the fitted growth function;
\(W'(t)\) denotes the instantaneous growth rate;
\(W''(t)\) denotes growth acceleration;
\(t\) denotes time or age;
\(y\) denotes the observed response.

The interpretation of each model-specific parameter is presented in the chapter on growth models.

3.6 Reporting problems
When reporting an error, include:

the PGM version;
the jamovi version;
the operating system;
the selected model and options;
the complete error message;
a reproducible or anonymized dataset, when possible.

4.8 Data coverage
A nonlinear growth curve should ideally contain observations from the early, rapidly growing, and late portions of the trajectory.
If the observed data do not adequately cover the upper part of the curve, the asymptotic parameter may be weakly identified and highly uncertain.
Early or truncated datasets may produce:

unstable asymptotes and geometric descriptors;
strong parameter correlations;
wide confidence intervals;
implausible extrapolation;
convergence failures.

Predictions beyond the observed time range should be treated cautiously, especially when the asymptotic phase is not well represented in the data.

5.3 Selecting time
The time variable must be numeric.
Its measurement unit determines the units of:

rate parameters;
inflection time;
critical-point locations;
derivative-based descriptors;
autocorrelation distances.

Changing time from days to weeks changes the numerical values of time-dependent parameters.

5.7 Recommended workflow
A defensible analysis commonly follows this sequence:

inspect the raw trajectories;
fit a biologically plausible mean curve;
assess parameter estimates and convergence;
inspect residuals versus fitted values and time;
evaluate heteroscedasticity;
account for repeated measurements using a subject identifier;
investigate residual autocorrelation;
compare candidate specifications;
verify parameter plausibility;
interpret derived growth descriptors only after accepting the underlying fitted model.

5.8 Multiple modeling setups
PGM allows multiple response-model configurations in the same analysis.
This can be used to:

compare candidate growth models;
compare variance structures;
compare response variables;
inspect alternative residual-correlation structures.

Comparisons should only be interpreted formally when the models use the same response observations and compatible likelihoods.

6.2 Mean, variance, and dependence
The complete statistical specification may contain three components:

Mean structure: the selected parametric growth equation;
Variance structure: the relationship between residual variance and fitted response;
Dependence structure: random effects and, optionally, residual autocorrelation.

These components address different features of the data and should not be treated as interchangeable.

7.2 Parameter units
The units of each parameter should be determined from the model equation.
Common interpretations include:

\(A\): response unit;
\(T_i\): time unit;
rate parameters: inverse time or a parametrization-specific rate unit;
shape parameters: dimensionless.

Parameters should not be compared directly across equations unless their mathematical definitions are equivalent.

8.4 Confidence intervals
Approximate 95% confidence intervals are first constructed on the transformed scale and subsequently back-transformed.
These intervals depend on asymptotic normal approximations and may be unreliable when:

the sample size is small;
parameters are weakly identified;
estimates are near parameter boundaries;
the fitted model is numerically unstable.





